==Spanish==
===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|es}} {{term|centum|lang=la}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|ḱm̥tóm|lang=es}}.

===Homophones===
* {{qualifier|depending on region}} [[siento]]

===Number===
{{infl|es|numeral|cardinal numeral||plural|cientos}}

# {{context|cardinal number}} [[one-hundred]] (100)

====Usage notes====
When used as an adjective before the modified noun, the [[apocopate]] form {{term|cien}} is used instead of {{term|ciento}}.

In composite numbers, the form {{term|cien}} is used before larger numbers components, {{term|ciento}} before smaller numbers.
: '''''cien''' mil pesos'' — “a hundred thousand pesos” ($100,000)
: '''''ciento''' noventa pesos'' — “a hundred ninety pesos” ($190)

When used substantively to indicate the number 100 itself, {{term|cien}} is more common in modern usage:
: ''los números de uno hasta el '''cien''''' — “the numbers from one to a hundred”
: ''hay dos '''cienes''' en el papel'' — “there are two hundreds on the paper” (two occurrences of the number one hundred)

In the indefinite sense, {{term|ciento}} is used:
: '''''cientos''' de pesos'' — “hundreds of pesos”

To indicate percentages, {{term|ciento}} is usually used, with regional exceptions, especially for 100%:
: ''cincuenta por '''ciento''''' — “fifty percent”
: '''''cien''' por '''cien''''' — “a hundred percent”
: '''''ciento''' por '''ciento''''' — “a hundred percent”

